# public/_redirects
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL
#
# Everything here could live in netlify.toml, and most of it already does.
# One rule does not work from there on this site: the rewrite that serves
# /sitemap.xml from the sitemap function.
#
# What was checked, rather than assumed:
#
#   /.netlify/functions/sitemap   →  correct XML, ~180 URLs. The function is
#                                    fine and always was.
#   /robots.txt                   →  correct plain text. So `force = true`
#                                    rewrites from netlify.toml ARE honoured.
#   /sitemap.xml                  →  the app's index.html, with or without a
#                                    cache-busting query. So the one rewrite
#                                    that points at a FUNCTION is the one
#                                    being skipped, and the /* catch-all
#                                    underneath it hands back the SPA.
#
# That is why Google has been indexing the home page in place of a sitemap —
# it asks for /sitemap.xml, gets an HTML page that is not a sitemap, and
# discards it.
#
# Netlify reads _redirects BEFORE netlify.toml, so a rule here is evaluated
# first and cannot be shadowed by the catch-all. The `200!` suffix is this
# file's spelling of `force = true`.
#
# The netlify.toml rule is deliberately left in place. If the toml one starts
# working again this one is simply redundant, and two rules that say the same
# thing are cheaper than finding out the hard way that the remaining one was
# the broken one.

/sitemap.xml    /.netlify/functions/sitemap    200!

# Same file under a path with no extension, so there is a URL to fall back on
# if anything ever shadows the .xml one again. robots.txt keeps pointing at
# /sitemap.xml, which is what search engines look for by convention.
/sitemap        /.netlify/functions/sitemap    200!